##### Article 1: Definition of Concepts

For the purposes of this order, the following terms shall have the meanings set forth below:
Woman: a person of the female sex.

Girl: a female child under 18 years of age.

Family: the nuclear family, the extended family, the family of the husband or wife, as well as the relationships, responsibilities and cultural ties that define identity and community.

Violence against women and girls: all acts of violence directed against the female sex which cause or are likely to cause physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering to women and girls, including threats of such acts, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, whether occurring in public or private life.

The violations include:

- Physical or moral, sexual and psychological violence exercised within the family such as beatings, marital rape, sexual assaults and offences, female genital mutilation as provided for by the Chadian Penal Code, forced or early marriages, "honour" crimes and other traditional practices harmful to women.

- Physical or moral, sexual and psychological violence exercised within the community including rape, sexual assaults and offences, sexual harassment, including cyber-harassment as provided for by the Chadian Penal Code and intimidation at work, in educational institutions and other places, procuring, trafficking in persons, forced prostitution.

Under this order, violence against women and girls also includes the failure of a medical or paramedical officer to provide a woman during childbirth with all due care, or to refrain from performing his or her professional duty.

Abortion: the use of means or substances intended to cause the premature expulsion of the foetus or, more generally, the artificial or voluntary termination of pregnancy in a woman;

Harassment: the act of giving orders, using words, writings, messages, gestures, and doing so repeatedly, uttering threats, imposing constraints, exerting pressure or using any other means for the purpose of obtaining from a person in a situation of vulnerability or subordination favours of any nature whatsoever, including sexual, for his or her benefit or for the benefit of a third party against the will of the harassed woman;

Procuring: the activity of one who facilitates the debauchery of others by acting as an intermediary, thereby deriving benefit from the fruits of this activity;

Forced sterilization: the act of committing or causing to be committed on a woman without her free consent or without a justified medical decision, an act likely to deprive her of her biological and organic capacity to reproduce;

Rape: any act of vaginal, anal or oral penetration by the sex of another or vaginal or anal penetration by any object without the intelligent and voluntary consent of the person penetrated. However, consent remains invalid, even deliberate consent, when it concerns girls under eighteen (18) years of age.

The person penetrated is not obliged to fight against his or her attacker. Being married to the person penetrated is not an excuse for the crime of rape;

Violence in the family environment: abuse of power or intentional neglect with a view to dominating, subjugating, controlling or assaulting women and girls physically, verbally, psychologically, patrimonially, economically and sexually, inside or outside the family home, whether the aggressor is or has been linked to his or her victim by family relations;

Domestic violence: any physical or sexual violence committed by a person against the person of another when the two parties are married, cohabiting, or consanguineous, or living in the same house, or when the two parties have had an intimate relationship in the past but are no longer together;

Economic violence: the use of one's means to slow down or prevent the economic or financial development of any person, or the prevention of any person from enjoying his or her socio-economic rights;

Patrimonial violence: any act or neglect affecting the survival of the victim and consisting of transforming, removing, destroying, withholding or misappropriating objects, personal documents, property and valuables, patrimonial rights or economic resources intended to cover his or her needs and which may extend to damage caused to property common to or owned by the victim;

Physical violence: any type of act resulting in non-accidental harm, through the use of physical force or any type of weapon or object that may or may not cause internal, external or both injuries;

Psychological or moral violence: the act of subjecting any person to repeated actions or words having the object or effect of degrading living conditions likely to infringe his or her rights and dignity, to impair his or her physical or mental health or to compromise his or her plans or future.

It is also an act or neglect causing harm to psychological stability, abandonment, repeated inattention, excessive jealousy, insults and humiliation, devaluation, marginalization, lack of affection, indifference, infidelity, destructive comparisons, rejection, restriction of self-determination and threats; all situations that may lead the victim to sink into depression, to isolate himself or herself, to lose self-esteem, or even to commit suicide;

Sexual violence: any act of a sexual nature having the effect of degrading or causing harm to the body and/or sexuality of the victim and which, consequently, infringes his or her freedom, dignity and physical integrity. It is the expression of an abuse of power dictated by a dominant position over the woman who is thus denigrated and treated as an object; all other similar forms which harm or infringe the dignity, integrity or freedom of women and girls;

Zoophilia: the act of any person by trickery, violence, threats or by any form of coercion or artifice, to have sexual relations or to force a person to have sexual relations with an animal.
